From 45e589118a63b7003083c46ea04584630fdff62c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 09:30:28 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 06/10] ppc64le: make the arch-hardcoded and page-size-bound
 tests arch-generic

Nine test files assumed x86-64 or arm64 in the string sense. Where the
test's intent is arch-neutral it now derives the expected value; where the
machinery genuinely only exists for x86-64/arm64 it skips with the reason.

Derived rather than hardcoded:

  os.test.js          os.arch() is checked against the value set Node
                      documents for process.arch, and against process.arch
                      itself, instead of a three-entry list.
  web-globals         navigator.platform is `Linux ${os.machine()}`, which is
                      what ZigGlobalObject.cpp's per-CPU() arms produce. The
                      old literal was already wrong for Linux arm64.
  process.test.js     process.release's asset name learns ppc64 -> ppc64le,
                      and the ICU floor table gains linux-ppc64.
  bun-upgrade         one releaseArch() helper replaces two copies of the
                      arm64 -> aarch64 map, and the fake release's asset list
                      covers ppc64le.
  run-crash-handler   the CPU-feature line has a ppc64le arm listing the
                      features the POWER8+ baseline guarantees, in the order
                      crash_handler/CPUFeatures.rs prints them.
  serve-file-slice-   supervisor.c gains a powerpc64 ptrace arm. glibc has no
  read-error          user_regs_struct there, so it reads pt_regs through
                      NT_PRSTATUS. Writing the negative errno into gpr[3] is
                      both necessary and sufficient: the kernel recomputes
                      CR0.SO from it on the way out of the syscall-exit stop,
                      so a positive EIO comes back to the tracee as a
                      successful 5-byte read.

Skipped with the reason, not made to pass vacuously:

  linker-order        functrace.c's own #error accepts linux x86-64/arm64 and
                      macOS arm64; the test's `supported` said "any linux".
                      generateOrderFile()'s gate had the same hole -- its
                      error message already named the architectures, but the
                      check did not -- so on any other linux it got past the
                      gate and failed while compiling the tracer. Both now
                      agree, which also makes the "refuses to run on an
                      unsupported platform" case run here instead of skip.
  gc-controller-      the "idle release lets FTL code age out" block needs an
  cadence             optimizing tier for there to be optimized code to age
                      out. Measured: with Baseline only, the run() options'
                      useEagerCodeBlockJettisonTiming drops the plain
                      baseline FunctionCodeBlocks during the warm-up, whatever
                      optimizedCodeAgingQuietSeconds says and however short
                      the loop -- `before` is 4, not the 40+ the fixture needs.

Two more, in the same spirit:

  build-rust lint     allRustTargets carries powerpc64le-unknown-linux-gnu,
                      which .buildkite/ci.mjs has no runner for, so "is
                      exactly the set of triples CI builds" failed. The
                      exception is now declared in rustTargetsNotInCI and
                      subtracted, and the lint checks each declared exception
                      is really in allRustTargets so it cannot outlive the
                      target it was added for.
  dead-code-escapes   bun_core::debug's PC_OFFSET is now #[cfg]-gated to the
                      architectures return_address() reads it on, instead of
                      carrying a powerpc64 #[allow(dead_code)]. The ELFv2
                      caveat moves to StackIterator::PC_OFFSET, where the
                      formula it warns about lives.

timer-gc-roots is the page-size one. RSS is accounted in whole pages and the
allocator's expansion steps are page-quantised with it: on the 64 KiB-page
POWER box a single step lands ~22 MB at once, four runs in five, and it does
not grow with the number of rounds -- granularity, not retention. An absolute
megabyte bound tuned for 4 KiB pages cannot tell that from the ~7 MB-per-round
leak the test guards against. harness gains pageSize(), and the test scales
both the workload and the bound by pageSize()/4096 so the leak stays the same
multiple of the bound on every host. 4 KiB hosts are unchanged.

Also: findRepoRoot() falls back to walking up from this file's own directory
when the walk up from cwd finds nothing. A --build-dir outside the repository
has no "bun" package.json above it, so ninja's regen rule -- which runs from
the build directory -- could not configure at all.
